Here's a complete example for a C# MCP server wrapping a REST API with stdio transport:

---

## Tool Class — `ApiTools.cs`

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get a user by their ID from the REST API.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUser(
        [Description("The numeric user ID to look up")] int userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching user {UserId}", userId);
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/users/{userId}", cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Search users by name. Returns matching results as JSON.")]
    public async Task<string> SearchUsers(
        [Description("Name or partial name to search for")] string query,
        [Description("Max results to return (1-50)")] int limit = 10,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/users?q={Uri.EscapeDataString(query)}&limit={limit}",
            cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }
}
```

---

## `Program.cs` — stdio transport

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC protocol channel
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

// Register HttpClient with a base address for your API
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<ApiTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK supports two injection styles:

| Style | When to use |
|-------|-------------|
| **Constructor injection** (non-static class) | Tools need shared services (`HttpClient`, `ILogger`, DB context, etc.) |
| **Method parameter injection** (static class) | Stateless tools that occasionally need a service |

**Key rules:**

1. **DI-registered services never appear in the tool schema.** The SDK distinguishes user-facing parameters (those with `[Description]`) from injected services automatically.

2. **Auto-injected types** — these are resolved from DI without any registration:
   - `CancellationToken` — cooperative cancellation
   - `IMcpServer` — send notifications/logging to the client
   - `IProgress<ProgressNotificationValue>` — report progress

3. **Constructor injection** (recommended for API wrappers):
   ```csharp
   [McpServerToolType]
   public class ApiTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
   ```
   The class is instantiated per-request with DI resolving constructor params.

4. **Method injection** (static classes):
   ```csharp
   [McpServerToolType]
   public static class Tools
   {
       [McpServerTool, Description("...")]
       public static async Task<string> Fetch(
           [Description("ID")] string id,
           HttpClient httpClient,          // injected from DI
           CancellationToken ct) { ... }
   }
   ```

5. **Registration** — register services in `Program.cs` as normal:
   ```csharp
   builder.Services.AddHttpClient<ApiTools>(c => c.BaseAddress = ...);
   builder.Services.AddSingleton<IMyService, MyService>();
   ```

`.WithToolsFromAssembly()` discovers all `[McpServerToolType]` classes via reflection at startup.
